WHAT THIS FORK CHANGED, AND WHY
===============================

1. POST /publish deleted.
   Upstream exposed an unauthenticated HTTP POST that accepted a cross-origin
   "simple request" (Content-Type: text/plain -> no preflight) with no Origin
   or Host check. [REAL] Executing that request against upstream returned
   {"ok":true} and the injected scripted HTML was then served at /. It was
   never used by the MCP path, which calls _publish() in-process, so it is
   removed rather than patched. The server now has no HTTP mutation route.

2. Host header validated.
   Only 127.0.0.1 / localhost / [::1], optionally with our port, are served;
   anything else gets 421. Upstream accepted any Host, so a remote page that
   had rebound a name to 127.0.0.1 could read the artifact back.

3. Untrusted content moved into a sandboxed iframe.
   Upstream served model-generated HTML as the top-level document with no CSP.
   Here `/` is a TRUSTED viewer we generate (banner + SSE) and the artifact is
   served separately at `/content`, framed with sandbox="allow-scripts" and no
   allow-same-origin, so it runs in an opaque origin under
   `connect-src 'none'`. SSE lives in the viewer, which is why the artifact
   itself needs no network permission at all.
   [REAL] Verified behaviourally, not by reading headers: honeypot sockets
   standing in for Ollama/Qdrant/LiteLLM/the internet recorded 0 connections
   from the artifact, while an identical page served without the CSP lit all
   of them.

4. Markdown is contained, not sanitized.
   marked v4.3.0 passes raw HTML through and upstream did not sanitize it, so
   Markdown artifacts executed script exactly like HTML ones. Rather than
   sanitize (which breaks legitimate inline HTML), Markdown now renders under
   the SAME sandbox+CSP boundary as HTML. Documented, not silently inherited.

5. file_path confined to an approved root.
   Upstream allowed any readable .html/.md/.txt anywhere on the host. The root
   is now the server's cwd at startup -- [REAL] Claude Code spawns stdio MCP
   servers with cwd = its own launch directory, so this is the active project --
   overridable with LOCAL_ARTIFACTS_ROOT. Paths are resolved (following
   symlinks) before the containment check, so a symlink inside the root that
   points outside it is refused.

6. State moved out of ~/.claude.
   Upstream wrote to ~/.claude/artifacts, a tree Claude Code owns. State now
   lives under $XDG_STATE_HOME/local-artifacts (0700, files 0600).

7. CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_ARTIFACT is no longer honoured.
   Upstream disabled its own publishing when that variable was set. It is
   Claude Code's switch for the HOSTED tool, and the whole point here is to
   work while the hosted tool is unavailable. Auto-open is controlled by
   LOCAL_ARTIFACTS_AUTO_OPEN instead.

8. Ported to the mcp 2.x API.
   Upstream targets mcp 1.x (@app.list_tools()/@app.call_tool()). mcp 2.x
   renamed FastMCP to MCPServer and dropped those decorators.

9. Model-visible strings rewritten in English.
   The tool description is the primary tool-routing signal, and local models
   are measurably sensitive to its wording.

10. Bind failure reporting tightened, then superseded by (13).
    publish() waited on an explicit ready event before reporting, so a bind
    failure could not race the first tool call. Production no longer binds in
    this process at all; the honest-failure property it added is kept by (13).

11. Deterministic diagram layout added (this fork only).
    The local benchmark showed the model hand-writing SVG coordinates and
    producing overlapping boxes and colliding labels. Architecture diagrams are
    now described semantically (nodes/groups/edges/kinds) and laid out by ELK,
    with an SVG design system supplying presentation. The model writes no
    geometry, and the emitted artifact contains no script at all.
    Mermaid is bundled and inlined by the trusted server, so diagram support
    never reintroduces a CDN dependency.

    Bundled: elkjs 0.12.0 (EPL-2.0 OR GPL-3.0-or-later, licences/elkjs-LICENSE.md),
    mermaid 11.17.2 (MIT), marked 4.3.0 (MIT). See vendor/VERSIONS.txt.

12. Productization (this fork only).
    - CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR is the authoritative project root; cwd is a documented
      fallback. [REAL] verified that stdio MCP servers receive it on Claude Code
      2.1.231, and that it equals the LAUNCH directory, not the git root.
    - Publishing writes the canonical source under <project>/.artifacts/
      automatically. For architecture that is the semantic graph, not the
      rendered SVG, so the artifact stays editable and reproducible.
    - artifact_id gives stable identity; the same id updates in place.
    - Tool renamed to mcp__artifact__publish (22 chars, from 39).
      [REAL] name mangling fell from 3/18 runs to 0/24.
    - Plugin packaging was tested and rejected: a plugin-provided MCP server is
      namespaced mcp__plugin_<plugin>_<server>__<tool>, which is LONGER, and
      tool-name length is the failure mode packaging would have been adopted to
      fix. Skills-dir plugins otherwise work (auto-load, MCP spawn,
      CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT/DATA) and need no settings.json entry.

13. Preview-server lifetime decoupled from the MCP process (this fork only).
    Upstream runs the HTTP listener as a daemon thread inside the stdio MCP
    server. Claude Code terminates that process at session end (MCP spec: close
    stdin, SIGTERM, SIGKILL), so every preview URL stopped answering the moment
    its session ended, while the artifact remained on disk -- the measured cause
    of "127.0.0.1 refused to connect". Upstream's README calls the server
    persistent; what persists there is the restored state file, not the server.
    This fork runs `server.py --serve` as ONE shared, session-independent
    process per machine, started on demand by the first publish and reused by
    every session after it, exiting after LOCAL_ARTIFACTS_IDLE_EXIT with no
    request and no connected viewer. It also fixes a second measured defect: a
    session that lost the bind race was poisoned for its entire life and could
    never publish, even once the port was free.
    Publishing crosses the process boundary through the 0600 state file, which
    is now written atomically -- deliberately NOT over HTTP, so the write
    endpoint removed in (1) stays removed.
    See docs/adr/013-artifact-preview-lifetime.md.
